# VaultaMark build-time configuration — template.
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your own values:
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored and must stay that way. Neither value below is a password, but both are
# specific to one Google Cloud project and one development machine, and neither belongs in a
# repository that may be published in full.
#
# Both are read by `vite.config.ts` via Vite's `loadEnv`, which also folds in matching variables
# already exported in the shell — so CI can set them as environment variables instead of writing a
# file, and a local file wins over the environment on a laptop.
#
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL. With this file absent, `npm run build` produces a package that
# installs, runs, and syncs through Chrome sync exactly as it should. The only thing it cannot do is
# offer Google Drive, and Settings → Sync says so in as many words.

# The OAuth client id for Google Drive sync, from your own Google Cloud project.
#
# Set up in docs/RELEASE.md §5: enable the Drive API, configure the consent screen for the
# `drive.file` scope and nothing wider (D22 — full `drive` is a Restricted scope requiring an annual
# paid audit), and create a Chrome-extension OAuth client for your unpacked extension id.
#
# With this unset the manifest omits the `oauth2` block **entirely** rather than emitting an empty
# one: Chrome treats a malformed `oauth2` as a manifest error and refuses to load the extension at
# all, so a source build with no Google project has to come out installable and Drive-less.
#
# VM_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com

# The base64 public key that pins the unpacked extension's id, from docs/RELEASE.md §5.4.
#
# The id an unpacked extension gets is derived from its path, so it changes when the folder moves —
# and an OAuth client is registered against one specific id. This freezes it.
#
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY, and enforced as such: `build/mv3-plugin.ts` passes it through for
# `npm run dev` (`--mode development`) and never for `npm run build`, because the Store assigns the
# real id and a `key` that disagrees with it breaks the upload. If you want a stable id while
# testing a production-shaped build, run `npx vite build --mode development`.
#
# VM_MANIFEST_KEY=MIIBIjANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAAOCAQ8A...
